From 0f7c32e5e19d0732d9d3f248c33c77fc6c256c73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:23:19 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 56/94] iio: common: qcom_smgr: check the AckNak of buffering
 responses

Only the QMI result of a buffering response was checked, so a request
SMGR refused (AckNak, TLV 0x11) looked accepted. As the stock sensors
HAL (SMGRSensor::processBufferingResp) does, take ACK_SUCCESS and
ACK_MODIFIED (logged at debug level) as success and anything else as a
refusal, except NAK_REPORT_ID on a DELETE, which is the answer for a
report that is already gone. Decode the reason pairs (TLV 0x12) for
the log.

A refused rate must not leave the sensor without reports: an enable
tries the current rate, then the default rate, each native rate and
the maximum, and keeps the first one SMGR takes. A DELETE no longer
zeroes the current rate, so a buffer disable keeps the user's rate.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SXjPGasJxD6XRYU1J9jsnW
---
